plied or stated in the problem, so the default linear model applies."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If the store uses tiered pricing, the per-pencil price might decrease for larger purchases. For example, 7 pencils at $2 ($0.286 each), but 21 pencils might be priced at $5 ($0.238 each) due to a bulk discount.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3","42f4de42693ac7ba8477a76c0d7c744d6e3fab3a2427611772125743f12b3819","86a78583e9d3bdc8fbfaf3a9ee3514b5fff76b0c3f426f01285d0f36241e948c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.1}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"bears_on_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_007","SRC_006","SRC_008"],"content":"Exploring the role of the covering principle in unit-rate problems. The standard answer is $6 via linear scaling (21 × $2/7). The skeptic stance (SRC_002/SRC_006) argues underdetermination, but the problem's domain implies uniform pricing. The discrete-bundle interpretation (SRC_008) yields the same $6 but via a different mechanism (3 bundles × $2). Key tension: is the covering principle an implicit assumption or an empirical expectation?","possible_next_move":"Clarify whether the problem domain standardly assumes linear uniform pricing, making $6 logically entailed, or whether it is an empirically defeasible expectation.","unfinished":"Does the unifier school (SRC_003) treat the covering principle as a domain convention or a logical necessity?","why_keep_this":"Captures the tension between mechanist, skeptic, unifier, and empiricist stances on a simple unit-rate problem."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Is the linear scaling principle a convention of the problem's domain, or does it require explicit statement to be logically valid?","related_refs":["NEW_001"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}